{"step_index":516,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-30T19:24:02Z","content":"Here is the brutal, honest truth: **No, you cannot get a 24GB VRAM GPU for ₹999 per month, and those specific gaming plans will not work for heavy AI video generation.**\n\nHere is why you have to be very careful with AI video:\n\n### 1. AI Video is a \"Monster\" Workload\nPlaying a video game like GTA 5 is easy compared to generating AI video. Local AI video generation (like LTX, Stable Video Diffusion, or Sora-style models) is the single heaviest task you can ask a computer to do. \n* To generate those 5-10 second clips smoothly, you absolutely *do* need a graphics card with **24GB of VRAM** (like an NVIDIA RTX 3090 or RTX 4090). \n* If you try to run local AI video models on a cheaper GPU with only 8GB or 12GB of VRAM, the software will simply crash with an \"Out of Memory\" error, or it will take 45 minutes just to generate a single 3-second clip.\n\n### 2. The Reality of the ₹999 & ₹3,000 Plans\n*   **AntCloud (₹999/mo):** This gives you a great GPU *for gaming* (usually an RTX 3060 or an A4000 with 8GB to 16GB of VRAM). It is not a 24GB card. It will struggle heavily or crash with AI video.\n*   **Xtreme Cloud PC (₹3,000/mo):** This usually gives you an RTX 3060 Ti or 3080. Again, great for gaming, but they do not have 24GB of VRAM. \n\nAn RTX 4090 (24GB) costs almost ₹2,000,000 (2 Lakhs) just to buy the card. No company will rent that out for ₹999 a month. \n\n### What You *Actually* Need for AI Video\nIf you want to rent a 24GB GPU in the cloud for AI video generation, you shouldn't use \"Gaming\" cloud PCs. You need to use **AI Compute Clouds**. \n\nThe standard platforms for this are **RunPod** or **Vast.ai**. \n*   **How they work:** You rent a massive 24GB GPU (like an RTX 3090) by the hour. \n*   **The Cost:** It usually costs about $0.35 to $0.45 per hour (around **₹30 to ₹40 per hour**).\n*   **The Math:** If you generate videos for 50 hours a month, it will cost you about **₹2,000**. If you use it for 100 hours, it will cost **₹4,000**. \n\n**Bottom Line:** If your goal is *only* GTA 5 modding, the ₹999 AntCloud plan is perfect. But if your goal is generating unlimited local AI video clips to stitch into 1-hour movies, the ₹999 plan will crash. You will need to use a pay-by-the-hour AI service like RunPod.","thinking":"**Assessing the Requirements**\n\nI'm now diving into the user's explicit request. My focus is centered on heavy AI video generation, especially with the mention of \"LTX 2.5.\" This clues me in on the user's desire to use models such as LTX-Video, which implies a need for substantial VRAM and local processing capabilities to produce long compilations.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Viability**\n\nI'm now investigating the feasibility of the user's hardware choices, particularly the cloud PC options. My thought process revolves around whether these services can actually deliver the necessary VRAM (24GB minimum) required for local AI video generation tasks with models such as LTX-Video. I've concluded that the ₹999/mo plan will not work, as it doesn't offer the necessary VRAM, based on a fact-check of current hardware offerings in cloud services. I'm now questioning if the ₹3,000/mo option will suffice.\n\n\n**Calculating the Costs**\n\nI've now crunched the numbers on cloud costs for 24GB VRAM GPUs like the RTX 3090/4090. Based on my research, even unmanaged Linux compute on platforms like RunPod and Vast.ai averages $0.30-$0.50/hour, which easily surpasses ₹999/month. Indian cloud services won't offer 24GB VRAM for such a low price. I've concluded that AI video generation requires more expensive enterprise-grade hardware.\n\n\n"}
